A||||6||Scott||1.6:1 Rocker arm questions||||1||
Z||000000||Scott||08-08-2001||02:36 AM||||Well, I read all the past posts I could find, and I'm left with a few questions:<BR>-Has anyone had fit problems using these rockers?  Specifically, w/ the valve covers & side/side spacing?<BR>-In one previous post a person had problems w/ the valve guides being excessively worn after using these rockers.  Has anyone else had such problems?  And, if known, how many miles were put on the engine before the damage became noticable? <BR>-Are high performance valve springs truely necessary for this application?<BR>  I plan on using Competition Cams roller tip rockers w/ factory rocker balls and studs.  I'll make sure each piston is at TDC, and pressurize the cylinder if I have to change the valve springs (and seals).  Does anybody have any further tips for this?<BR>TIA<BR>Scott    ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Leadfoot||08-08-2001||09:51 AM||||From what I have read here and at other sites there should be no fit problems and changing to high performance valve springs is not necessary.  However, you should expect increased valve guide wear.   I have read from one guys post that he used the rockers out of a 305 that were 1.6 and had no problems.  He also stated that you should see a 10% increase in HP throughout the entire power range.  I personally think this is over exaggerated.  I am about to change over to these rockers myself but have more pressing mods that I want to do.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dave Gunsul||08-08-2001||03:04 PM||||You most certainly do need high perf. valve springs! If you use the stockers you will bind!<BR>As for the valve guide wear: I have never heard such a thing on any car that's used higher ratio rockers before so I wouldn't worry about it to much. I would suggest that this was caused by improper install and/or wear in. Any rocker arm that's adjusted incorrectly can cause problems.<P>HP gain: I wouldn't expect to much form this<BR>mod, probably less than 10 HP.<BR>Every little bit helps though!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||4
Z||000003||Scott||08-08-2001||03:44 PM||||Thanks for the input guys.  I'm not expecting much extra HP from it.  I'm just hoping it breates better in the higher RPMs.  I'm mainly doing it in preparation for a T3 I'm gonna install this winter.<BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000004||Leadfoot||08-08-2001||06:09 PM||||Dave, I don't know if you read some of the archives but there is a lot of contradicting info.  You have one person saying that they installed the rockers with the stock springs and had no problems.  In addition the issue of increased valve guide wear was brought up and I am not sure which one to believe.  Here is a site which I got my information from<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/engine-rockers.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/engine-rockers.html</A> <P>This forum is great but sometimes you have people on here argue so well that they can sell an eskimo a freezer.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dave Gunsul||08-08-2001||08:20 PM||||The valve guide wear issue is a non issue. Many new cars come with larger ratio rockers today from the factory and don't have this problem. The Mustang Cobra for instance comes with Crane 1.7 roller rockers. (Fords<BR>have 1.6's normally)(this was the 5.0 Cobra that i'm referring to BTW)<P>The binding issue: I have read a couple-a articles where guys have tryed the 1.6 rockers on the 2.8 and have had bind in the upper RPM's. The stock V6 springs are supposed to be strong enough for this upgrade but they're not, especially when you factor in the age and mileage of these cars.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000006||PJ||08-08-2001||10:14 PM||||I think I've used the 1.6 rockers with the stock springs without a problem (hey, it was a long time ago). The guide wear issue rears it ugly head when you combine higher ratio rockers with bigger cams and other mods. You then need to start measuring the ideal pushrod length that will keep the contact point of the rocker centered on the valve stem tip. I'm now up to 6.55" pushrods using a Crane H272-2 cam (their biggest hyd. cam) with 1.6 stamped small block rockers. The guides are still original after four rebuilds.<P>PJ<BR> ||||reg||1
